      "Name": "body",
      "Html": "\u003Cstyle class=\u0022code-analysis-syntax\u0022\u003E\n.docs-content pre{overflow-x:auto;margin:1rem 0 1.75rem;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;border:1px solid rgba(20,42,72,.16);border-left:4px solid #0e6fae;border-radius:4px;background:#f7fbfe;color:#10263a}\n.docs-content pre code{white-space:pre;color:inherit;background:transparent;font-size:1rem;line-height:1.65}\n.language-csharp .syntax-comment{color:#587287;font-style:italic}.language-csharp .syntax-string{color:#8a3d12}.language-csharp .syntax-keyword{color:#6542a6;font-weight:700}.language-csharp .syntax-number{color:#096b65}.language-csharp .syntax-type{color:#0a6680;font-weight:600}\n\u003C/style\u003E\u003Cheader class=\u0022docs-page-header\u0022\u003E\u003Ch1\u003E[page[title]]\u003C/h1\u003E\u003Cp\u003EA broker may have at most one external or exposure dependency.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/header\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EWhat The Standard says\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u0026ldquo;Up \u0026 Sideways\u0026rdquo; \u0026mdash; Brokers sit at the integration edge and may depend only on the external native capability they wrap, never on services or neighboring brokers. \u003Ca href=\u0022https://github.com/hassanhabib/The-Standard/blob/master/1.%20Brokers/1.%20Brokers.md#127-up--sideways\u0022 target=\u0022_blank\u0022 rel=\u0022noreferrer\u0022\u003Eread it here\u003C/a\u003E.\u003Cbr\u003E\u0026ldquo;One Resource\u0026rdquo; \u0026mdash; The Standard requires each broker to represent exactly one external resource. Limiting a broker to one external or exposure dependency enforces that resource boundary. \u003Ca href=\u0022https://github.com/hassanhabib/The-Standard/blob/master/1.%20Brokers/1.%20Brokers.md#128-one-resource-one-broker\u0022 target=\u0022_blank\u0022 rel=\u0022noreferrer\u0022\u003Eread it here\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EWhy it matters\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EA broker is the application\u0027s narrow boundary to one external concern. Multiple external dependencies in one broker couple failure modes and configuration together, making the boundary harder to replace, test, and protect independently.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EBad code example\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode class=\u0022language-csharp\u0022\u003E\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-comment\u0022\u003E// Violates STXB001: A broker may have at most one external or exposure dependency.\u003C/span\u003E\n\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-keyword\u0022\u003Eclass\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EStudentBroker\u003C/span\u003E(\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EIStudentDb\u003C/span\u003E db, \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EIAuditApi\u003C/span\u003E audit) { }\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EGood code example\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cpre\u003E\u003Ccode class=\u0022language-csharp\u0022\u003E\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-comment\u0022\u003E// Follows STXB001\u003C/span\u003E\n\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-keyword\u0022\u003Eclass\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EStudentBroker\u003C/span\u003E(\u003Cspan class=\u0022syntax-type\u0022\u003EIStudentDb\u003C/span\u003E db) { }\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E"
    }
